{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PyEAQgTrHYK7tNGGs5Nta2","type":"tool_result","content":"380c44155 WI-956: checkpoint reconcile: the whole set against the settled code (GPT Terra, resumed), and the RESYNC entry's untracked-file sentence\n077bec24d WI-956: counted round 3 filed (convergence stance): REVIEW-A (Codex Sol) and REVIEW-B (Claude Opus, high, stand-in), MINORs only\nfc168c5ef WI-956: convergence map recorded before counted round 3 (wi-956 D-005)\n1600:detail = \"\"\"An approval that records what it blessed by COPYING the registries needs no canonical text to hash, no separator that cannot occur in a cell and no second exclusion list, so no digest engine sits behind this row — the comparison basis is discharged by split_changed_cells(csv_path, id_col, head, row) -> {\"approved\": {cell: (before, after)}, \"traced\": {...}}: the id column and Status are excluded STRUCTURALLY - the id is the join key rather than content, and folding Status in would make every flip read as an amendment and every amendment invisible behind its own flip - and the remainder is split by spine_cell_class, the SAME classifier the amend-without-flip guard reads, so the two can never disagree about what normative means. It is PUBLIC for exactly one reason: baseline_snapshot.is_drifted reads it as the drift basis (LLR-173), so the snapshot comparison and the staged-amendment guard are one rule with one home rather than two rules that agree until they do not. _APPROVED_TEXT names the one Status value whose row TEXT is approved (Approved — the status fold collapsed Verified and Planned into it) and requires the SAME value on both sides, so prose rewritten under an Approved row is never invisible. It returns the before/after PAIRS a brief has to render anyway, which is why it replaced a hash rather than being wrapped by one. Needs are compared row by row through the same basis, their approved and traced cells split like every other tier's. ONE TWO-TREE WALK feeds every reader that asks what a spine delta did: _spine_row_sides pairs each row's before and after side once, and four consumers read it — staged_spine_amendments (the rows whose approved text moved), staged_approval_acts (the rows that crossed INTO an approval claim, or arrived already making one), staged_drafted_rows (the rows a lane authored below approval, which the first-approval mint is raised over) and lane_approval_refusal, which words the merge slot's approval-act refusals. The walk is shared rather than copied so that the set one reader EXEMPTS is the set another REPORTS, MINUS the de-approvals: an Approved -> Drafted withdrawal moves Status, so the amendment reader exempts it, but it blesses nothing, so the approval-act reader does not report it and staged_drafted_rows raises the re-approval it now owes instead. Subject to that one subtraction, an approval act is invisible to the amendment reader by construction, and a row that no reader saw is unrepresentable. The per-row judgement is stated once, in _approval_act, so the flip arm and the born arm are read side by side rather than as two branches of the walk that could drift apart. UNREPRESENTABLE WITHIN A DECLARED BOUND, and the bound is named rather than left as an absence — the walk's universe is a PARAMETER over two declared sets, not one set every reader shares. SPINE_CSVS is the THREE registries whose rows carry an id column and an approved text — system-requirements, low-level-requirements, test-cases — and is the walk's default, so staged_drafted_rows, and the first-approval mint over it, read exactly it. APPROVAL_ACT_CSVS is those three PLUS stakeholder-needs and the assumptions registry's two tiers (assumptions and surrogates), and staged_approval_acts passes it, so the merge slot requires each approval act, including one on a need, to have an accepted verdict that judges its own rows. The need tier is inside because it carries the same status vocabulary and every approval act requires the same independent verdict. An unreadable claimed frontmatter is refused by name at the merge slot and at partial close rather than read as an unscoped lane; a lane that records no scope is judged only by its acted rows and copies, with no `Adjudicates` wording. AMENDMENT_CSVS is an alias of APPROVAL_ACT_CSVS rather than a second list, and staged_spine_amendments passes it, so the amend-without-flip warn and the intake amendment mint, which both consume that walk, cover the need, assumption and surrogate tiers beside the three; the warn's Hat-Refs arm stays structurally silent on those three tiers, none of which carries Hat-Refs. The three registries a snapshot anchors that no approval reader walks — interfaces, external, components — are listed in OUTSIDE_THE_APPROVAL_ACT: off-spine, their approval cells governed by their own rung rather than this one. The two lists are pinned as one exhaustive, disjoint statement, SNAPSHOTTED == APPROVAL_ACT_CSVS + OUTSIDE_THE_APPROVAL_ACT, against baseline_snapshot.SNAPSHOTTED's eight. A tier added to the snapshot therefore lands on one side or the other by a deliberate edit, and cannot reach no approval reader at all.\"\"\"\n1997:rationale = \"\"\"SN-005's strongest clause is that the enforcement floor is git plus CI running the SAME harness a human runs, and that clause was true, shipped, and pinned by nothing: the existing workflow tests asserted triggers, action digests and job names, and one asserted that the reference workflow mentioned the harness AT ALL. The measurement that made it checkable is that \"the same harness\" cannot mean one command, because the human bar is itself tiered by MOMENT - smoke per commit, the full suite at a slice close, the release tier at a tag - so the obligation is per-moment equivalence and the mapping is the thing that has to have one home. What this deliberately does NOT claim, and the honest split is the row's: it says nothing about an adopter's edited copy, which is outside this repo's reach, and it proves one definition of passing per moment rather than equivalence on all inputs, which is not mechanizable at any price.\"\"\"\n2189:code_symbol = \"RECORD_PREFIXES/fold_listing/tree_identity/refresh_subject/refresh_attestation/format_bar_green/bar_attestation/tests_digest/mechanical_close_attestation/work_tip/governing_rev/governing_identity/format_trailer/parse_trailer/round_file/session_log/format_scope/gating_span/filing_refusal/branch_paths/logged_rounds/round_entries/branch_entries/tree_already_judged/declared_phases/phases_owed/round_count/format_branch_trailer/branch_trailers;attest/_attest_preflight/_attest_message/_lane_off_trunk/_harness_floor_refusal/_attest_commit/_bar_refusal/_review_bar_command/_run_review_bar/_unstaged_dirt\"\n2470:detail = \"is_observation_tc(tc) reads the Automated cell alone: a case whose Automated reads No is an observation test case, the one marker covering every judgment the harness cannot rerun, since the Level and Verification cells disagree across rows. An inputs entry is a repository path or a registry row id (a tier prefix, a hyphen and digits, as SR-184), the id standing for the row's cells as the observation writer's digest reads them; an id is inside the repository by construction and is never judged as an escaping path. observation_tc_findings(tcs) returns (failures, advisories): an observation case with no inputs, no max_age, or, when it carries Assumption-Refs, no sampling policy is an advisory naming the row; a max_age below MAX_AGE_FLOOR_DAYS or not a whole number, an inputs entry naming a path outside the repository (input_escape: absolute, drive-qualified, or still climbing out with `..` once normalized, so `docs/../src` is inside), a sampling value outside SAMPLING_VALUES, a sample_size that is not a whole number of at least one, an acceptance_rule that is empty or whitespace, and one of the two model cells without the other, are failures composed into the always-on integrity class naming the row; an automated case carrying any of these cells is an advisory. sampling_model_declared(tc) is true only for a sampled case carrying both model cells valid; whether the rule justifies the claim is judged when the case is approved.\"\n2481:detail = \"A kitlib module that imports no sibling script, in the pattern of kitlib/evidence.py. One TOML file per record under docs/test/observations/, named <TC id>.<observed-at in UTC, colon-free>.toml, so records written on different lanes never collide. FIELDS = (tc, outcome, observed_at, provenance, expires, judged); OUTCOMES = (pass, fail); `judged` is the inputs digest and is the empty string for a test case declaring no inputs. parse_utc accepts only the canonical form YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ. parse(text) returns None unless every field is present, observed_at and expires parse through parse_utc, expires is not earlier than observed_at, provenance is non-empty after trimming, and outcome is in OUTCOMES, so a truncated or hand-mangled file is never a result. read_records(root) skips any name with a leading dot, which is the temporary name write_atomic writes before os.replace; write_atomic retries a Windows PermissionError on the replace a bounded number of times, then raises. latest(records, tc) returns the newest record by observed_at.\"\n2492:detail = \"record_observation.py --tc TC-### --outcome pass|fail --by <provenance> [--expires <UTC>] resolves the test case from the registry, refuses an undeclared test case or one that is not an observation test case, an outcome outside OUTCOMES, a missing provenance, and an expiry later than observed_at plus the case's max_age; with no --expires it writes observed_at plus max_age. inputs_digest(root, inputs) folds each declared input in declared order as name, NUL, SHA-256 of its LF-normalized bytes, the fold kitlib/stage uses; a registry row id is digested as that row's cells as the carrier reads them. The writer calls write_atomic and never opens the test-case registry for writing. bootstrap.MAPPING ships the script and the kitlib module; the directory is not a declared stage input, so recording a sample never makes the stage's fingerprint or the release evidence stale.\"\n2662:detail = \"A sibling of consolidate.py imported by intake. observation_test_cases(root, rev) reads observation cases at a revision. checkpoint_for(root, rev, tc) returns the case's Trigger when it names the release or stage-gate checkpoint, else merge. due_cases(root, rev, now, checkpoint) reads committed inputs and records and uses the cadence decision to apply the configured closed-WI floor and declared trigger. A case with no record is due at once, and an expired record is due at once; neither waits for the floor. Without a trigger, a changed input digest is due subject to the floor; without inputs, only absence and expiry ma
[... byte cap hit — full stream in out/run-logs/ ...]
 detection, the verdict gate, the missing-bar refusal, the window audit and one end-to-end run, satisfying SR-156 via LLR-140 -> after: the same, plus the attest operation. It commits only a verified review attestation, and only after the bar and named-tests command that its lane's staged tree declares. It refuses before the bar on hooks off, on the primary checkout holding the branch, on a dirty worktree (untracked files included), on an unavailable declaration, on a harness-floor refusal, and on a message that is unreadable or carries the label. A hook refusal or rewrite is undone, leaving the branch and index unchanged. A bar that moves the index or worktree off its staged tree is refused with the index restored. The line joins the trailer block. A review-only tip is refused at the merge seam. Expected now also cites LLR-207's attest, producer and refresh-attestation contract -> not the same: a whole new route enters the verified set, and a new LLR enters Expected. I would bless it. Each arm is observable and maps one for one onto LLR-207's new producer and attest clauses and onto the producer and merge bullets of the Done-when. Its \"the declaration is unavailable\" is the right closed reading of the spec's Trust ruling (absent file, section or key; empty value; unparseable file).\n- [MEANING] LLR-207 Detail -> before: refresh_attestation recognizes a station refresh from its subject, parent, tree and a `Bar-Green: tree= work=` trailer; there is no bar, no producer and no attest route -> after: one column-0 grammar carrying `bar=<review|landing|no-bar>` and `tests=<64 hex|none>`, verified against the commit's own tree and parent. Refresh recognition is restricted to landing and no-bar, and the walk still steps through an empty review carrier. One producer fixes the staged tree, refuses a red or tree-moving bar, restores the index on failure and joins the trailer block. The attest route refuses on its six named preconditions and reads its lane's own declarations with no default. A trailer without `bar=` is not read -> not the same: a closed bar set, a required field, a new producer and a new CLI route are all new obligations, and an old-grammar refresh that peeled before now does not. I would NOT bless this text as it stands, for one reason. The design row states the `tests=` field only as `<64 hex|none>` and never says what the 64 hex is a digest OF. The spec defines the field as \"a digest of the sorted named test paths, or `none`\" (Deliverable 1) and gives it its own Done-when bullet: the same set in any order gives the same digest, and a different set a different one. WI-957's dispatch check will compare exactly that value across producers. Today TC-205 drives the normalization, de-duplication, order-independence and empty-set arms under `verifies = LLR-207`, against a contract LLR-207 does not state. So a refactor that changed the digest (unsorted, unnormalized, a different hash) would keep this row true and break the cross-lane comparison, and the verified arm has no requirement home. The rest of the amendment is sound and I would bless it: the walk-through clause is correct, the refusal set is closed, and no symbol name is added beyond the cell's existing ones. Three possessives are also missing (\"the commit own tree\", \"the attested lane own declarations\", \"the message final trailer block\"), which a reader could stumble on. This row is NOT re-anchored. Because this rung is RELEASED, the refusal binds only LLR-207: TC-205 and TC-132 sit in test-cases.toml, a different registry, and their re-attestation does not wait on it.\n\nVERDICT: MEANING rows=3\n\n## Dispositions\n\n```toml\ntitle = \"WI-956 LLR-207: state the Bar-Green tests digest's contract, then re-sit the amendment\"\nworkstream = \"process\"\nsr_refs = [\"SR-156\"]\nneeds = [\"WI-956\"]\nsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\nbuildtier = \"strong\"\n```\n\nThis is spine text only; no code change is called for. In LLR-207's Detail, state what `tests=` carries, 